Maintainer idea (2026-11-04): Skeid becomes a frame. A deployment writes its own Skeid class (subclass or role) that carries its dispatch logic — e.g. ask a GPU manager where a model is loaded, wake it, queue or refuse when the card is full — and Skeid offers one simple step to turn that class plus Skeid into a single deployable file.
Why: every deployment-specific decision so far became a generic config key (route_column, routed_only, node_source, documents) and a capability-pinning dance downstream (capability pins in downstream deployments). A class keeps generic Skeid generic.
Today: usage hooks are subclassable (documented); Proxy->new accepts skeid => $obj; route.plan/route.next/pick_node are methods but no contract, and synchronous (cannot wait on an outside answer); bin/skeid hardwires Langertha::Skeid->new; ADR 0019 decision 3 rejected a packed binary (PAR measured: equal request path, +0.2/3.7 s start, unpacks 80 MB, needs PAR_VERBATIM=1 and explicit namespaces, still links glibc/libssl).
Scope: ADR 0030 (proposed) first, then implementation:
1. Dispatch hook contract: documented, versioned methods a subclass may override (plan, pick, admit/refuse, on_finish), an async variant for pick (promise) so a class can consult an outside service without blocking the loop (ADR 0005), defaults = today's behaviour byte for byte.
2. skeid serve --class My::Skeid [-I lib] and config key skeid_class; Proxy uses it.
3. skeid pack --class My::Skeid [-I lib] -o my-skeid: one file. Reference via PAR::Packer with what ADR 0019 recorded; supersedes ADR 0019 decision 3 for this purpose; packaging stays optional, the image stays the default deployment.
Tests: subclass example under t/ (hook overrides change routing; default unchanged), CLI --class, pack smoke test as release/author test (needs PAR::Packer).


